{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-puzzle-solving", "file": "references/heterogeneous-demo-reconciliation.md", "content": "# Heterogeneous demonstrations: reconcile before transferring

When the same instance supplies demonstrations with widely different dimensions, background colors, seed colors, and motif shapes, do not assume the visible seed in the test is enough to derive a familiar operation. First record each pair's input/output changed-cell mask, foreground/background roles, and unchanged seed/background cells. Search for one role-level invariant that explains all pairs; explicitly reject candidate rules such as zero-component flood fill or global recoloring unless they reproduce every output exactly.

After each failed submission, revisit the transformation hypothesis rather than changing arbitrary thresholds or component definitions. If the demo cap is exhausted, use remaining submissions only for a rule supported by the full evidence. This instance's guesses of connected-background recoloring were not demonstrated by cross-pair replay and failed; the reusable lesson is to validate cross-demo invariance before committing, not to favor another particular rule.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-rnd0/frozen/seen/play/users/u067/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-puzzle-solving/references/heterogeneous-demo-reconciliation.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-puzzle-solving", "file": "references/periodic-stripe-sequence.md", "content": "# Periodic stripe grids: global-sequence hypothesis

When demos are made of uniform-color rows or columns, reason over the 1D stripe sequence as well as individual cells. Compare the input stripe sequence with the full output sequence, including repeated periods and boundary phase. A transformation may generate a periodic color sequence from a compact set of stripe exemplars rather than preserve or locally extend the visible rows. Extract the exact sequence from every demo, test candidate periods and phase, and verify every output stripe before transferring to the test. Do not extrapolate from a single visually suggestive sample; request another demo if the rule connecting input stripe order to output period remains ambiguous.
